WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.300
This is a quick field guide to setting up a DroneScout Bridge with RID2Caltopo.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.100
The bridge receives Remote ID broadcasts that a phone or tablet may not reliably receive on its own.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.585
It then relays those observations over Bluetooth,

00:00:17.585 --> 00:00:20.900
so RID2Caltopo can display and record the aircraft.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:33.755
For a typical field deployment, bring a DS-100 bridge and antenna, a USB-C cable, a charged battery bank,

00:00:33.755 --> 00:00:40.500
a phone or tablet running RID2Caltopo, and a known Remote ID aircraft for the pre-flight check.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.400
Before field use, check for current firmware and confirm wireless relay mode.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.873
Our RID2Caltopo field profile uses WLAN reception, balanced scanning,

00:00:54.873 --> 00:00:59.700
US channels 1 through 11, inserted self-ID, and relay ping on.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.700
Relay ping lets RID2Caltopo confirm that the bridge is powered before flight.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.900
Save the settings and return the bridge to normal operation.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.500
Line of sight matters more than a mast.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.000
Part 107 pilots normally keep the aircraft in visual line of sight,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.300
so placing the bridge near the controller is usually enough.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.500
In authorized beyond-visual-line-of-sight operations,

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:29.477
the controller still needs a reliable command and telemetry path, even when the aircraft may operate up to a mile away.

00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:34.100
Use a mast or tripod only when a little elevation helps clear local obstruction.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.800
Do not pair the bridge in the tablet's Bluetooth settings.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.700
Just power the bridge and open RID2Caltopo.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.700
In wireless relay mode, the bridge automatically rebroadcasts observations in a format the app can receive.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.100
Keep the app device within about 10 meters of its bridge.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.200
Confirm that Remote ID monitoring is active.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.800
Launch a known Remote ID aircraft safely.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.700
The bridge light should flash when it detects an aircraft.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.500
In RID2Caltopo, confirm the aircraft appears.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.400
Its position and timestamp continue updating.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.400
And, when configured, its track reaches the incident map.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.200
A taller mast cannot see through a mountain ridge.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:32.000
For rough or mountainous terrain, deploy multiple R2C stations, where each station has a bridge and the RID2Caltopo app.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.800
Network the stations through R2C Tracker so observations can be coordinated across the incident.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.300
Place each station where it has a useful radio view of its assigned flight area.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:56.500
Before operations begin, confirm power, the antenna is vertical, useful line of sight, scanner status,

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.600
live aircraft updates, and tracker or map publishing.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.500
RID2Caltopo supports situational awareness,

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.500
but always visually verify that a received track corresponds to the aircraft in the field.

